/E (Without access to a regulation F1 in Schools track, our team had to approach physical testing through alternative yet effective methods. Our process was centred around three major components. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [16 17]
>>
endobj
598 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [18 19]
>>
endobj
599 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Second, we conducted in-depth physical scrutineering using precision tools\(vernier callipers, gram weight scale, screw gauge\) to confirm that all critical dimensions, angles, and tolerances matched our CAD model, reducing the risk of disqualification and ensuring aerodynamic accuracy. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [20 21]
>>
endobj
600 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Finally, to validate our digital simulations, we collaborated with SGSITS, our local engineering college, to carry out a wind tunnel test. This allowed us to compare our CFD predictions with real-world airflow behaviour, giving us valuable insight into the performance of our aerodynamic features and enabling final refinements to the car design.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
601 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (MANUFACTURING)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [35]
>>
endobj
602 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This page talks about the Assembly and Final finishing process including surface finishing, painting, glueing etc. It also mentions Workplace Safety measures and Quality Analysis: How we ensured our car model met our standards.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [36 37]
>>
endobj
603 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (sanded for improved fit and finish. Although the VMC-machined wheels were highly precise, minor edge inconsistencies were corrected using 1000-grit sandpaper to ensure smooth rotation and balance.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
604 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Painting & Sealing:)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
605 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 859 0 R
/E (Each part of the car was painted individually to reduce weight by avoiding hidden areas and to ensure greater precision. The entire primer and paint process was carried out by a professional company, Lords of Detailing, equipped with specialised tools for a smooth and uniform finish.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
606 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 860 0 R
/E (Before priming, the car body was sealed with a mix of Fevicol and water to prevent polyurethane foam from reacting with hydrocarbon-based primers and paints. A coat of plastic primer was then applied to all parts and left to dry for 2 hours, chosen for its strong adhesion and protection against corrosion and cracking.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
607 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 861 0 R
/E (Spray paint was applied according to the colour scheme, using masking tape to separate sections. After drying, waterslide decals formed the livery, and vinyl stickers were used for the 'STEM Racing' logo, as per regulations. A clear coat was added to seal the surface and protect it from moisture. No paint or primer was applied to attachment spaces, as they were dimension-critical.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
608 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Assembly:)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [40]
>>
endobj
609 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (After painting, Loctite SF 770 primer was applied to all joints and left for 1 minute to activate the inert Nylon 12 surfaces. Loctite 406 was used for larger joints, while Loctite Precision was applied to small gaps like wheels and bearings. This ensured strong, reliable bonding. Attachment spaces were included in the car body, with space left for glue to securely fix outsourced parts like the front wing, rear wing, and wheel system.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [41]
>>
endobj
610 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (WORKPLACE SAFETY:)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [39]
>>
